## Motivation

The paper addresses the challenge of uncertainty quantification in machine learning models, particularly in the context of predictive uncertainty quantification. Traditional methods often focus on conditional expectations and are limited by the law label process. Tolerance intervals and sets, which offer probabilistic bounds on the range of values that a random variable may take, are crucial for risk assessment and model evaluation. These bounds are typically derived from i.i.d. data, but their properties—such as expected width and coverage—have been less explored. Previous works have shown that conformal prediction with polynomial-time wrappers can control the expected width but typically do not minimize it. This paper aims to control the expected width of coverage sets while achieving the desired coverage, thereby providing a more efficient and effective way to quantify and reduce predictive uncertainty. The significance of this research lies in its ability to improve the efficiency of uncertainty quantification, which is essential for building trust in models and evaluating their performance in practical applications.

## Main Idea

The paper introduces a novel approach to improve the efficiency of uncertainty quantification in machine learning models by controlling the expected width of coverage sets. The key innovation is the use of Bayesian quadrature to derive width-sketching algorithms, which are algorithms with guaranteed expected width. The paper proves a uniform upper bound on the width of a width-sketching algorithm and provides an instance where the width in expectation is twice as large as the optimal width. To address this limitation, the paper introduces the width-optimality notion and an approximate version termed mean-width optimality. This allows for the derivation of algorithms with the desired coverage while minimizing the mean width. The paper also provides a high-level perspective on the relationship between width-sketching algorithms and depth-sketching algorithms, showing that they provide complementary forms of coverage, and demonstrates the application of the framework to conformal prediction with Bayesian quadrature.

